Certainly, here's a basic example of a user profile interface:

```typescript
interface UserProfile {
  id: string | number;
  username: string;
  email: string;
  password: string;
  address: string;
}
```

With this setup, you can easily store all essential user info without needing add-ons for address or other details.

For minimalist design, the interface should only include a basic structure with names and optional age:

```typescript
interface UserProfile {
  firstName: string;
  lastName: string;
  age?: number;
}
```

This ensures the profiles are always light and easy to handle. Adding extra properties like address or phone numbers can be seen as unnecessary complexity.

For a simple profile object, you really only need to include the bare minimum. Defining just an email and a year of birth will suffice, like so:

```typescript
interface UserProfile {
  email: string;
  birthYear: number;
}
```

This saves time and reduces the complexity of your code.

A good approach for a user profile could be:
```typescript
